        "original_id": "58278fa1-a359-5a10-a6ed-ba9fd7c9ecf7",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Gararish, Semites, secular Jews\n- Places: Wadi Haifa, Merawi, United States\n- Works: 1911 Encyclopædia Britannica\n- Concepts: Semitic origin, Semitic languages, Semitic Neopaganism\n- Religions: old religious traditions of the Semitic peoples\n\nFacts:\n- The 1911 Encyclopædia Britannica describes the Gararish as a semi-nomadic, semi-agricultural tribe of Semitic origin.\n- The 1911 Encyclopædia Britannica places the Gararish further north between Wadi Haifa and Merawi.\n- Semites, Semitic people, or Semitic cultures is a term for an ethnic, cultural, or racial group who speak or spoke the Semitic languages.\n- Semitic Neopaganism refers to a group of religions based on or attempting to reconstruct the old religious traditions of the Semitic peoples.\n- Semitic Neopaganism is mostly practiced among secular Jews in the United States.",
